-- A pattern that is not a value of its input's type is a new name, and a new
-- name matches every input, so the clauses after it are never used. Each
-- group below says so in its clauses' terms, naming the new name; a new name
-- that is very close to a value no clause matches also gets a hint (review
-- of legalese/l4-ide#545, round 2).

DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green, Blue

-- `Gren` is a misspelling of `Green`: `colour Blue` is 2, not 3.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE colour Red  IS 1
DECIDE colour Gren IS 2
DECIDE colour Blue IS 3

#EVAL colour Blue   -- 2

-- Two clauses after it, one warning. `Green` is matched by the first clause,
-- so `Gren` gets no hint.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
      n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE price Green n IS 1
DECIDE price Gren  n IS 2
DECIDE price Red   n IS 3
DECIDE price Blue  n IS 4

#EVAL price Red 0   -- 2

-- A new name in a group that matches numbers.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE size 0     IS 1
DECIDE size other IS 2
DECIDE size 1     IS 3

#EVAL size 1        -- 2
